[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Event Dispatching and Forwarding in Svelte"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Svelte provides a clean, reactive model for component communication. While props allow data to flow downward from parent to child, events allow data and signals to flow upward from child to parent. In this tutorial, you will learn how to dispatch custom events from child components and forward native or custom events through intermediate components."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Prerequisites"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Basic understanding of Svelte component structure (.svelte files)."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Node.js and npm installed on your machine."},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Part 1: Dispatching Custom Events"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"To send an event from a child component to a parent, Svelte provides the createEventDispatcher function. This function returns a dispatcher that you can call with the event name and an optional detail payload."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"<script>
  import { createEventDispatcher } from 'svelte';

  const dispatch = createEventDispatcher();

  function handleClick() {
    dispatch('increment', { delta: 1 });
  }
</script>

<button on:click={handleClick}>
  Increment
</button>"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, the Child.svelte component dispatches an event named 'increment' containing a detail object with the delta property."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Listening to Dispatched Events in the Parent"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"The parent component can listen to this custom event using the standard on: directive, just like a native DOM event. The payload is accessible via the event.detail property."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"<script>
  import Child from './Child.svelte';

  let count = 0;

  function handleIncrement(event) {
    count += event.detail.delta;
  }
</script>

<Child on:increment={handleIncrement} />
<p>Current Count: {count}</p>"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Part 2: Event Forwarding"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Often, you have a component that wraps a native element or another component, and you want the parent to listen to events directly from the inner element. Instead of manually creating a dispatcher and re-emitting every event, Svelte allows you to forward events simply by using on:eventname without a handler."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"<!-- CustomButton.svelte -->
<button on:click>
  <slot />
</button>"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"By writing <button on:click>, you instruct Svelte to forward any click event that happens on the internal button upward to whatever component consumes CustomButton."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Consuming Forwarded Events"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Now, the top-level parent can listen to the click event on CustomButton as if it were a native element."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"<script>
  import CustomButton from './CustomButton.svelte';

  function handleClick() {
    alert('Button clicked!');
  }
</script>

<CustomButton on:click={handleClick}>
  Click Me
</CustomButton>"},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"Event dispatching and forwarding are powerful patterns in Svelte. Dispatching lets you communicate state changes upward with custom payloads, while event forwarding reduces boilerplate when wrapping standard HTML elements or nested components."}]
